1) What's the trade-off between bias and variance? 

A model is said to be biased when its predictions are not "centered" around groud truth, even if the distance between ground truth and the average prediction isn't large. A model is said to have high variance when the small changes in the input lead to large changes to the predrictions, even if those are nicelly "centered" on the ground truth. In classic machine learning, simple models (eg linear regression, logistic regression) might become biased but won't display high variance. Highly parameterized models (eg polinomial regression, random forest) might introduce high variance. The trade-off is then to find a model that is well-parameterized enough to not be biased while not too parameterized to introduce high variance.

2) What is gradient descent?

Gradient descent is the most popular algorithm used for training neural networks. In its core, gradient descent is the application of the chain rule. During trainign of the neural network all operations in the forward pass must have a well-defined derivative, the loss is computed by comparing the model prediction to the label, gradient descent computes the derivative of the error (thus the gradient) for every model parameter during the backward pass. Those gradients are used to optimize the model parameters in the updating step such to minimize the cost function.

3) Explain over- and under-fitting and how to combat them?

Under-fitting will usually happen when the model is not parameterized enough for the task. The model fails to learn features that are representative of the task. The output of the model is an average that minimizes the cost function without great predictive value. This is often visible in classical machine learning that makes use of fairly simple models such as linear regression. 

Overfitting is the opposite phenomenon, where a model is overparameterized for the task. The model may memorize examples from the training data without learning features that generalize for new data points. Typically, this is visible by plotting the train and validation curves for a given metric (eg the loss), and at a certain moment in the training, the training loss continues to decrease while the validation loss starts to increase. Overfitting happens often when training neural networks on relatively small datasets.

4) How do you combat the curse of dimensionality? 

It is possible to make a model more expressive by adding new input dimensions. In the case of tabular machine learning, those can be seen as new features with potential predictive power. However, the search space for the optimal parameters of the model grows exponentially with the number of new features (dimensions) and the data space becomes exponentially sparser. There are techniques for combating it. Dimensionality reduction pre-processing, such as t-NSE or a simple PCA. Feature engineering, such as combining two or more features into one more expressive feature. Filtering non-relevant or spurious features by performing a feature importance analysis. 

5) What is regularization, why do we use it, and give some examples of common methods?

Regularization is an additional cost function that is typically used to constrain the parameter search space. It can be used to prevent overfitting by penalizing large parameter values. Or it can be used to increase inference time efficiency; L1 regularization on parameter norms is a method used for pruning models of parameters that reach values close to zero. 



45) What is vanishing gradient? 

Vanishing gradients is a phenomenon observed when training deep neural networks. The magnitude of the gradient left for the initial layers of the network isn't large enough to facilitate training. That is specially problematic when activation functions such as sigmoid and tanh are used inside the network without skip connections. This issue is less present in modern neural networks, techniques such as batch normalization, layer normalization, activation functions such as ReLU (and variants), and skip connections mitigate the issue of vanishing gradients during training.

46) What are dropouts?

Dropout is a technique where the activations are "droped" by a certain probability. This is a regularization techinique used to avoid overfitting, though it slows down training. The inference is deterministic, the activations are weighted by the dropout probability. Dropout can be used to model unceartainty, and has been widely used for baysian modeling. 

55) What’s the difference between a generative and discriminative model?

A generative model models the data probability P(x, y) or P(x | y) and a discriminative model models the likelihood probability P(y | x). Discriminative models learn the decision boundary between classes, SVMs, NN classifiers and regressors are discriminative models. Generative models learn to "generate" data given a class label or other input, Nayve bayes, mixture of gaussians, VAEs, GANs and diffusion models are examples of generative models.

56) Instance-Based Versus Model-Based Learning.

Instance-Based is also known as non-parametric models. Duting optimization the model selects informative points present in the data for minimizing a cost function. During inference the model will make predictions based on the selected points. k-NN is an classic example.

Model-based is also known and parametric. During optimization the parameters of the model are adjusted to minimize a cost function. During inference, those parameters are used for making predictions. Linear regression, neural networks are examples.

58) What is the difference between LDA and PCA for dimensionality reduction?

Those are both linear dimensionality reduction algorithms. PCA stands for Principal components analysis, mathematically the algorithm identifies most important  eigenvectors that describe a set of data points and selects the most important ones. Intuitively, it finds the dimensions that align with the highest variability in the data. LDA stands for Linear discrimnant analysis, it is a supervised algorithm for dimensionality reduction. LDA looks for the projection that maximizes the distances between classes and minimizes the distances in intra point-classes. LDA is restricted to reduce the dimensionality to 1 - number of classes.  

